### 3.1 Learning Generalizable Driving Representations from Video Generators

World models such as Genie [[8](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2512.23421#bib.bib8)] and Cosmos [[2](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2512.23421#bib.bib2)] learn real-world structure by training on large-scale video generation, and many studies [[72](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2512.23421#bib.bib72), [23](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2512.23421#bib.bib23)] indicate that video generators internalize physical regularities and act as strong zero-shot learners. In autonomous driving, real scenes provide virtually unlimited video while dense annotation is costly, so we propose to learn driving representations from large-scale driving video generation, akin to how humans acquire driving competence. Concretely, let ℰ\mathcal{E} be the video encoder and zz the latent representation of a clip x0x\_{0} with z=ℰ​(x0)z=\mathcal{E}(x\_{0}). A generic denoiser produces a latent denoising trajectory {zt}t∈𝒯\{z\_{t}\}\_{t\in\mathcal{T}} under conditioning cc via a single-step update

