#### Implementation Details.

Our DriveLaW model consists of a 2B video DiT, initialized from LTX-Video [[24](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2512.23421#bib.bib24)] pretrained weights, and a 133M diffusion planner for trajectory planning. We enable DriveLaW to acquire both video generation and planning capabilities through a three-stage training pipeline consisting of video pretraining followed by action fine-tuning. In the video pretraining stage, we train the Video DiT [[56](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2512.23421#bib.bib56)] on 8​Hz8\,\mathrm{Hz} frames from nuScenes [[9](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2512.23421#bib.bib9)] and NuPlan [[10](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2512.23421#bib.bib10)] with a two-stage curriculum. We first establish temporal coherence by training on low-resolution, long-duration clips (740×352×121740\times 352\times 121 frames). Then we fine-tune the model on high-resolution, shorter clips (1280×704×251280\times 704\times 25 frames). First two stages are trained for 30k iterations with a batch size of 4, a learning rate of 1×10−51\times 10^{-5}, and a weight decay of 5×10−25\times 10^{-2}. We use flow matching [[50](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2512.23421#bib.bib50)] with token-wise uniform σ∈[0,1]\sigma\in[0,1], so each latent token interpolates independently between data and noise. In the trajectory fine-tuning stage, we feed the past four camera frames and supervise 2​Hz2\,\mathrm{Hz} trajectory points over the next 4s, updating both the Video DiT and the Planning DiT. We use a batch size of 192 for 44k steps with a fixed learning rate of 3×10−53\times 10^{-5} and a weight decay of 1×10−51\times 10^{-5}. At inference, we use 30 sampling steps for video generation and 5 steps for trajectory planning.

